window.STAGES=[{"id":"orbit","title":"An annual land fingerprint","alt":"20,000 km","lines":[{"id":"orbit.1","who":"narrator"},{"id":"orbit.2","who":"narrator"},{"id":"orbit.3","who":"narrator"}]},{"id":"sensors","title":"Radar and optical satellites","alt":"790 km","lines":[{"id":"sensors.1","who":"narrator"},{"id":"sensors.2","who":"narrator"},{"id":"sensors.3","who":"narrator"}]},{"id":"dpixel","title":"Learning from satellites","alt":"10 m","lines":[{"id":"dpixel.1","who":"narrator"},{"id":"dpixel.2","who":"narrator"},{"id":"dpixel.3","who":"narrator"}]},{"id":"inference","title":"Scanning the planet","alt":"all land","lines":[{"id":"inference.1","who":"narrator"},{"id":"inference.2","who":"narrator"},{"id":"inference.3","who":"narrator"}]},{"id":"tasks","title":"Finding and labelling places","alt":"3 km","lines":[{"id":"tasks.1","who":"narrator"},{"id":"tasks.2","who":"narrator"},{"id":"tasks.3","who":"narrator"},{"id":"tasks.4","who":"narrator"},{"id":"tasks.5","who":"narrator"},{"id":"tasks.6","who":"narrator"}]},{"id":"missions","title":"Measuring forests by regression","alt":"the world","lines":[{"id":"missions.1","who":"narrator"},{"id":"missions.2","who":"narrator"},{"id":"missions.3","who":"narrator"}]},{"id":"yours","title":"Explore the fingerprints yourself","alt":"anywhere","lines":[{"id":"yours.1","who":"narrator"},{"id":"yours.2","who":"narrator"}]}];
window.TRIPS=[{"id":"sentinels","stage":"sensors","after":"sensors.2","title":"How the Sentinels work","src":"A","refs":[["A","#S3.SS1","CVPR paper, Section 3.1"]],"text":["Sentinel-2 is a pair of satellites orbiting seven hundred and eighty-six kilometres above the Earth. Each one photographs a strip two hundred and ninety kilometres wide, in thirteen bands of light. The sharpest bands show detail down to ten metres. Together, the pair cover the equator every five days.","Sentinel-1 carries a radar and orbits six hundred and ninety-three kilometres up. It sends out microwave pulses and measures two kinds of echo. One, called VV, mostly shows the surface of the ground. The other, called VH, mostly shows leaves and branches.","Both satellites belong to the European Union's Copernicus programme. Their data are free for anyone to use."]},{"id":"barlow","stage":"dpixel","after":"dpixel.3","title":"How Barlow Twins training works","src":"AF","refs":[["A","#S3.SS3","CVPR paper, Section 3.3"],["F","#S4","TESSERA v2, Section 4"]],"text":["Barlow Twins is a form of self-supervised learning. That means the model learns from the data itself, without anyone labelling it. Training has two goals.","The first goal is consistency. Tessera takes two random samples of clear observations of the same place, from different days of the year. It learns to give both samples nearly the same fingerprint. So a place gets the same fingerprint, whatever the weather was on the days it was seen.","The second goal is variety. Each part of the fingerprint should record something different, rather than repeat another part. Tessera checks this across a large batch of places from all over the world.","To check the second goal, training uses a much larger version of the fingerprint, which is thrown away when training ends. The researchers found that making this part bigger did not help. The extra effort was better spent on a bigger model and more data."]},{"id":"forty","stage":"dpixel","after":"dpixel.3","title":"How many observations does it use?","src":"AF","refs":[["F","#A4","TESSERA v2, Appendix D"],["A","#A1.T6","CVPR paper, Table 6"]],"text":["Some places are seen clearly only a handful of times a year. Others are seen dozens of times.","In training, Tessera sees just eight or sixteen observations of a place at a time, chosen at random. That teaches it to recognise a whole year from a few glimpses.","When it makes the fingerprints, it uses every clear observation. Places with a similar number of observations are processed together, so none is thrown away and none is repeated."]},{"id":"storage","stage":"inference","after":"inference.1","title":"How the fingerprints are stored","src":"ACF","refs":[["A","#S3.SS4","CVPR paper, Section 3.4"],["F","#A23.T28","TESSERA v2, Table 28"]],"text":["Each fingerprint is stored as a hundred and twenty-eight whole numbers, from minus a hundred and twenty-seven to a hundred and twenty-seven, plus one scale for the square. Multiplying them together gives the fingerprint back.","The model was trained with this rounding in mind, so it loses very little: less than one point of accuracy in the researchers' tests. At full precision, one year of the planet would take a petabyte to store. Stored this way, it takes a quarter of that.","The newest fingerprints put their most important values first, so a map that needs to save space can keep just the first sixteen, an eighth of the size, and keep about ninety-two per cent of the accuracy."]},{"id":"distil","stage":"inference","after":"inference.1","title":"How a large model trains a small one","src":"F","refs":[["F","#S5","TESSERA v2, Section 5"],["F","#A21","Appendix U"]],"text":["Tessera is trained in two steps. First, a large model with two billion weights learns from about fourteen billion d-pixels. It is accurate, but running it over the whole planet for one year would take about a hundred years of work on a single powerful graphics processor.","So a much smaller model, with forty-four million weights, is trained to give the same fingerprints. It does the same job with about two years of that work, and scores almost as well.","The training also puts the most important information first. The first sixteen values of each fingerprint must rebuild the large model's fingerprint on their own, and so must the first thirty-two and the first sixty-four. Like a set of nesting dolls, each shorter fingerprint sits inside the longer one."]},{"id":"cosine","stage":"tasks","after":"tasks.2","title":"How similarity is measured","src":"D","refs":[["D","","tze on GitHub"]],"text":["Each fingerprint can be drawn as an arrow in a space with a hundred and twenty-eight dimensions. To compare two fingerprints, Tessera's tools measure the angle between their arrows. This is called cosine similarity.","Arrows pointing the same way score one. Unrelated arrows score close to zero. On this page, a square counts as similar if its arrow is within about thirty-two degrees of the one you chose."]},{"id":"umap","stage":"tasks","after":"tasks.2","title":"How the UMAP cloud is made","src":"C","refs":[["A","#A1.F16","CVPR paper, Figure 16"]],"text":["Nobody can picture a hundred and twenty-eight dimensions, so the cloud is a simplified map, made with a method called UMAP.","First, UMAP links each square to the fifteen squares with the most similar fingerprints. Then it places all the squares in three dimensions and adjusts them step by step, pulling linked squares together and pushing the others apart.","Squares that sit together in the cloud really are similar. The distances between the groups are less reliable. The cloud on this page shows four thousand squares of Cambridge."]},{"id":"labels","stage":"tasks","after":"tasks.4","title":"How many labels does a map need?","src":"EF","refs":[["E","","tessera-eval on GitHub"],["F","#S5.F4","TESSERA v2, Figure 4"]],"text":["A program called tessera-eval measures this. Near Vienna, it trains a classifier on more and more labelled pixels of each crop, and tests it each time. Its accuracy climbs quickly at first, then levels off.","The test matters too. Pixels next to each other in a field look almost identical, so a test on random pixels flatters the model. A tougher test holds back whole fields that the model has never seen. With a thousand labelled pixels of each crop, the random test scores zero point nine three, and the unseen fields zero point six five.","When Tessera was compared with other systems given one per cent, thirty per cent or all of the labels, its lead was widest with one per cent."]},{"id":"unet","stage":"tasks","after":"tasks.6","title":"How the U-Net finds solar farms","src":"DF","refs":[["D","","tze on GitHub"],["F","#A16","TESSERA v2, Appendix P"]],"text":["The solar model is a U-Net, a type of neural network shaped like the letter U. The first half shrinks a patch of fingerprints in steps, so the network can see the shape of a whole solar farm.","The second half enlarges it back to full size. Links across the U carry the fine detail over, so the edges stay sharp. This network has about forty-one thousand weights, and it runs in your browser."]},{"id":"heads","stage":"missions","after":"missions.2","title":"Which small model goes on top?","src":"BF","refs":[["B","","Applications paper, Section 2"],["F","#A16","TESSERA v2, Appendix P"]],"text":["The small model on top is called a head. The simplest is a vote among the nearest labelled examples, as in the classifier on this page. Researchers also use random forests, and small neural networks of one to three layers.","For a label, the network ends by choosing a class. For a number, it ends with a single output. For shapes, it reads a patch of fingerprints, such as sixty-four by sixty-four squares, with a network such as a U-Net.","The choice of head matters. On a set of city mapping tasks, Tessera came a close second with a head that saw one square at a time. With a small head that saw the neighbouring squares as well, it came first."]},{"id":"compare","stage":"missions","after":"missions.3","title":"How Tessera compares with other models","src":"F","refs":[["F","#S5.SS1","TESSERA v2, Section 5.1"],["F","#A24.F9","Figure 9"]],"text":["The researchers compared Tessera with other systems that turn satellite data into fingerprints, including Google DeepMind's AlphaEarth. They tested them on fourteen mapping tasks that played no part in building Tessera, from tree species to road density.","Tessera scored best overall, with a model at least seven times smaller than its nearest rivals. Its lead was widest when only one per cent of the labels were used.","The newest fingerprints also remove most of the stripes that earlier ones showed along the satellites' paths, and they stay steady from year to year where the land hasn't changed."]}];
